## The registry

The API keeps the registry in Postgres. Writes need the token; reads do not.

| Route | What it does |
|---|---|
| `POST /v1/experiments` | Registers a design under its plan hash. Registering the same design twice returns the same experiment id. |
| `POST /v1/experiments/{experiment_id}/result` | Posts a result against the registered plan hash; refused when no experiment has that id, when the experiment has no plan hash, or when the hash does not match. |
| `GET /v1/experiments` | Every registered experiment with its results. |
| `GET /v1/experiments/{experiment_id}` | One experiment with its results. |
| `GET /v1/audit` | The audit log; every write adds its row there before its response is sent. |

When the API does not answer, the pipeline registers locally and says so in the registry file, as
it did in this build. The site's recorded session (`web/public/data/recorded_session.json`)
replays this design and its difference in differences result through the API code running against
a local Postgres, and a separate Windows client checked the live registry with its own verification
experiment (`docs/serving.md`). No record in the repository shows this design registered on the live
registry itself.

## The email experiment (Hillstrom)

Kevin Hillstrom's MineThatData file is a real randomized test: {{ v('data.hillstrom.rows') }} recent
buyers of a catalog retailer, assigned in equal thirds to no email
({{ v('email.arm.control') }}), a men's merchandise email ({{ v('email.arm.mens') }}) or a women's
merchandise email ({{ v('email.arm.womens') }}) in 2008, with visits, conversions and spend recorded
over the {{ v('email.window_days') }} days that followed. The design was hashed and registered before
the analysis ran (plan hash `{{ v('email.plan_hash') }}`, through the `{{ v('email.registered_via') }}`
route), with the men's email as the primary arm and the correction for segments stated in advance.

The sample ratio gate {{ v('email.srm_passed') }} (p = {{ v('email.srm_p') }}). Effects are treated minus
control with {{ v('email.level') }} intervals. The men's email raised conversion by
{{ v('email.mens.conversion.effect') }} of customers ({{ v('email.mens.conversion.lower') }} to
{{ v('email.mens.conversion.upper') }}) and spend by {{ v('email.mens.spend.effect') }} per customer
({{ v('email.mens.spend.lower') }} to {{ v('email.mens.spend.upper') }}); at a {{ v('email.margin_on_spend') }}
margin on that spend and {{ v('email.cost_per_email') }} per email, each email earned
{{ v('email.mens.profit_per_email') }}. The women's email earned {{ v('email.womens.profit_per_email') }}.

{{ table('email.effects') }}

{{ label('email.effects') }} Visit and conversion are rates as fractions of customers; spend is
dollars per customer.

**Segments, with Benjamini-Hochberg.** The effect is estimated in each of the
{{ v('email.segments.mens.conversion.count') }} purchase history segments, and the p values are corrected
by {{ v('email.segments.correction') }} at the false discovery rate set in `marginal_experiments.email`.
On conversion {{ v('email.segments.mens.conversion.significant') }} of the
{{ v('email.segments.mens.conversion.count') }} segments stay significant after the correction; on spend,
{{ v('email.segments.mens.spend.significant') }}.

{{ table('email.segments.mens.conversion') }}

{{ label('email.segments.mens.conversion') }}

